MewStage third_party - VENDOR NOTICE
====================================

This directory holds third-party source vendored into the MewStage repository. Every vendored component keeps its
upstream license file. MewStage itself is MIT (../LICENSE).

Provenance and updates are data-driven. The authoritative pins (one commit per upstream repository, with the
archive and content hashes), the per-component layout, the MewStage-authored files the updater never touches, and
the patch series with its categories all live in third_party/vendor.lock.toml:

    python third_party/tools/vendor.py                      # re-vendor every component at the pinned commits, every patch
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --check              # verify the tree equals the lock + the recorded patch set
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --list               # sources and components
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --list-patches       # every patch, its category and its purpose
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --skip-category X    # vendor without an optional category (docs/vendoring.md)
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --emit-svg-generated # refresh MewUI.Svg/Generated (needs dotnet)
    python third_party/tools/vendor.py --self-test          # test the patch applier

The updater downloads GitHub codeload tarballs of the pinned commits, reads them in memory (no extraction, no git,
no `patch` binary) and applies the patches with a strict pure-Python applier that fails loudly when a patch no
longer applies (the signal that upstream moved the patched code). The applied patch set is recorded in
third_party/.vendor-state.toml. Design and procedures: ../docs/vendoring.md.

Layout: third_party/MewUI and third_party/MewVG mirror the upstream REPOSITORY layout (build/, src/<project>/,
extensions/<project>/). Upstream project files are used as shipped; MewStage hooks in only through upstream's own
local-override file build/MewUI.Local.props and the same hook in MewVG (the mewvg/build patch below).

